: 44% occupancy</title></rect><text x="537.3" y="155.6" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#5a5346" text-anchor="middle">44%</text><text x="537.3" y="284.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#8a8276" text-anchor="middle">Oct</text></g><g class="bar"><rect x="572.3" y="175.3" width="31.4" height="92.7" rx="3" fill="var(--brass)"><title>Nov: 38% occupancy</title></rect><text x="588.0" y="170.3" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#5a5346" text-anchor="middle">38%</text><text x="588.0" y="284.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#8a8276" text-anchor="middle">Nov</text></g><g class="bar"><rect x="622.9" y="185.0" width="31.4" height="83.0" rx="3" fill="var(--brass)"><title>Dec: 34% occupancy</title></rect><text x="638.6" y="180.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#5a5346" text-anchor="middle">34%</text><text x="638.6" y="284.0" font-family="DM Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#8a8276" text-anchor="middle">Dec</text></g></svg><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:.75rem;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem"><caption style="text-align:left;font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d675d;margin-bottom:.4rem">Typical short-term rental occupancy by month (illustrative)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col" style="text-align:left;padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--navy)">Month</th><th scope="col" style="text-align:right;padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--navy)">Occupancy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jan</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">34%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Feb</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">35%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Mar</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">40%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Apr</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">44%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">May</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">48%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jun</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">52%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jul</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">54%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Aug</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">53%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Sep</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">46%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Oct</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">44%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Nov</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">38%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Dec</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">34%</td></tr></tbody></table><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.74rem;color:#6d675d;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.5rem">For contrast: this is the seasonal swing that beach short-term rentals carry. Conway's long-term, student-driven rentals avoid this curve entirely, which is the point.</p></figure><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:8px;padding:1.5rem 1.6rem;margin:2rem 0"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.62rem;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:1rem">Short-term rental data &middot; Conway</p><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(112px,1fr));gap:1.2rem 1rem;margin-bottom:1.15rem"><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">$21,285</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">average annual revenue</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">34.7%</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">occupancy</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">$245</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">average nightly rate</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">106</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">active listings</div></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.87rem;line-height:1.65;margin:0">The strongest tenth of listings here gross $5,347 a month or more; the median listing makes $1,808. June is the strongest month and January the weakest. Averages cover every listing in the market, including part-time ones, so a well-run property can sit well above them. <a href="/invest/airbnb-income/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Compare all nine submarkets</a>. <span style="font-size:.78rem">AirROI, data through 2026-08-08.</span></p></div><h2>How rental returns work in Conway</h2><p>Conway is a cash-flow market, and the math is refreshingly simple. Lower purchase prices against steady long-term or student rents produce strong cash-on-cash returns when the property is bought right. Near campus, rent-by-the-room can lift gross income above a conventional family lease, though it raises turnover and management costs. This is also the most natural BRRRR market on the Strand: buy below market, renovate, rent, and refinance on the improved value.</p><h2>Financing an investment property in Conway</h2><p>Conway finances cleanly. Conventional single-family homes are standard collateral, DSCR loans qualify on rent for the long-term-rental strategy, and the lower purchase prices keep down payments accessible, which is part of what makes the BRRRR model work here. The main lender concern is flood: properties in or near the Waccamaw floodplain will need flood insurance, and that cost belongs in your numbers from day one.</p><h2>Conway rules and costs that move the numbers</h2><p>Conway's costs are simple, with one exception. South Carolina assesses non-owner-occupied property at 6 percent versus 4 percent for a primary residence, and Chapter3 will confirm the rental's tax line up front. The exception is flood: properties in or near the Waccamaw floodplain require flood insurance, and on a thin-margin cash-flow deal that premium can swing the return. Pull the flood determination and a real insurance quote on every address before you commit, because the rents here do not leave room for surprises.</p><h2>The Conway investor thesis</h2><ol class="thesis-list"><li><span class="thesis-num">01</span><span class="thesis-text">Long-term and student rental, not vacation rental, is the thesis. Conway is about year-round cash flow.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">02</span><span class="thesis-text">Coastal Carolina University provides a renewing tenant base and a rent-by-the-room upside near campus.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">03</span><span class="thesis-text">Lower entry pricing against steady rents produces the cash-flow and BRRRR profile many investors prefer.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">04</span><span class="thesis-text">Predictability replaces seasonality. There is no summer cliff to plan around.</span></li></ol><div style="margin-top:1.6rem;display:flex;gap:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/invest/long-term-rental/">Run a Conway address through the analyzer</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="/contact/">Talk to a Conway agent</a></div><h2>What a first Conway deal looks like</h2><p>A classic first Conway deal is a modest single-family home bought below market, either held as a long-term rental or run as a <a href="/invest/student-rentals/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">student rental near Coastal Carolina University</a>. Lower purchase prices against steady rents produce the cash-on-cash returns the beach markets struggle to match, and the buy, renovate, rent, refinance approach works here better than anywhere on the Strand. The one non-negotiable is flood: check the zone and price insurance into the deal before you write the offer, every single time.</p><h2>What to watch before you buy in Conway</h2><ul style="padding-left:1.1rem;margin-top:.5rem"><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>Flood risk.</strong> The Waccamaw River has flooded parts of Conway. Check the flood zone, elevation, and insurance on every address before you offer.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>Student-rental management.</strong> Student tenants mean higher turnover and more wear. Price management and maintenance into your numbers.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>Proximity premium.</strong> Walk-to-campus homes command better student rents. Location relative to the university drives the rent-by-the-room math.</li></ul><h2>Where Conway sits</h2><p>Conway is the Horry County seat, about 15 miles inland from Myrtle Beach on the Waccamaw River, which is part of the Intracoastal Waterway system. It is a historic river town of roughly 20,000 residents with a walkable downtown and Riverwalk, and it is home to Coastal Carolina University and Horry-Georgetown Technical College. It is not a beach town, and that shapes everything about its real estate.</p><h2>The Conway market right now</h2><p>Conway is one of the more affordable Horry County submarkets and currently favors buyers. Redfin reported a median near $280,000 for the three months ending May 2026, down about 4.5 percent year over year, with days on market around 110. Other trackers put the median around $300,000 to $320,000. Either way it is below the Horry County average. See the <a href="https://www.redfin.com/city/4264/SC/Conway/housing-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Redfin Conway market page</a>. Inventory ranges from 1940s historic homes downtown to new-construction subdivisions and active-adult communities such as Lakeside Crossing and Myrtle Trace. <!-- TIMMY: current CCAR MLS median and DOM, and the strongest value subdivisions. --></p><h2>Short-term rental rules, and why Conway is a long-term play</h2><p>Short-term rentals are allowed under City of Conway and Horry County rules plus any HOA restrictions, but Conway is not an oceanfront vacation market, so the real rental demand here is long-term. Demand comes from Coastal Carolina University students and staff, Horry-Georgetown Tech, and a year-round county-seat workforce, which supports steady annual leases rather than peak-summer nightly rates. Average rents have been around $1,800 a month. For most investors, Conway is a long-term-rental and appreciation play, not an Airbnb play. <!-- TIMMY: which Conway areas are strongest for CCU student rentals and workforce LTR, and typical rents by type. --> Every jurisdiction's licenses and taxes: <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">short-term rental rules by city</a>.</p><h2>Owning in Conway: the 4 percent versus 6 percent property tax rule</h2><p>South Carolina assesses an owner-occupied primary residence at a 4 percent ratio, and it does not pay the school operating tax. A second home or investment is assessed at 6 percent and pays the school operating tax. In the city of Conway that makes a second home's tax bill about 2.5 times a primary home's on the same house. See the <a href="https://dor.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Carolina Department of Revenue</a> for the assessment-ratio rules. Full county math, with a calculator: <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Horry County property taxes</a>.</p><h2>Flood zones and insurance in Conway</h2><p>Flood risk is the single biggest practical consideration in Conway, because the Waccamaw River runs through downtown. Conway saw serious river flooding after Hurricane Matthew in 2016 and, more severely, Hurricane Florence in 2018, when the Waccamaw crested at record levels and inundated parts of the city for an extended period. Some areas sit in flood zones that require flood insurance. Pull the FEMA flood map and the property's flood history before you buy anywhere near the river. <!-- TIMMY: which neighborhoods flooded in 2018, which stayed dry, and current premium ranges. --> What the three coastal policies cost: <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">coastal home insurance costs</a>.</p><h2>Conway lifestyle and amenities</h2><p>Conway offers an Americana river-town lifestyle: a preserved historic downtown, Riverfront Park and the Riverwalk, festivals through the year, and Coastal Carolina University events and sports. There are a dozen city parks, golf nearby, and Waccamaw River boating, fishing, and kayaking. Daily shopping and dining are well established, and the beach is about a 20 to 25 minute drive east.</p><h2>Schools serving Conway</h2><p>Public schools fall within the Conway attendance area of <a href="https://www.horrycountyschools.net/about-hcs/our-schools" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County Schools</a>. Conway is also home to Coastal Carolina University and Horry-Georgetown Technical College. Assignment is by address, and Chapter3 will confirm the exact schools on the district boundary map.</p><h2>What it is like to live in Conway</h2><p>Conway is an inland river town about 15 miles from the beach, with a preserved historic downtown, Riverfront Park and the Riverwalk on the Waccamaw River, and the energy of Coastal Carolina University and Horry-Georgetown Tech. It offers more house and land for the money than the beach towns, a real downtown, and a dozen city parks, with the ocean a 20 to 25 minute drive east.</p><p>For a live-in buyer, the biggest practical consideration is flood. The Waccamaw River flooded parts of Conway after Hurricanes Matthew (2016) and Florence (2018). Chapter3 will check the FEMA map and the flood history for any address near the river. Housing ranges from 1940s historic homes downtown to new-construction subdivisions and active-adult communities.</p><h2>Buying in Conway as a primary residence</h2><p>Conway is not a vacation-rental market; it is a primary-residence, appreciation, and long-term-rental market driven by the university and a year-round county-seat workforce. For a live-in buyer that means value, space, and a genuine community rather than seasonal swings. A primary residence is taxed at 4% with the owner-occupant credit; a second home at 6% without it.</p><h2>Read the Conway HOA rules before you commit to a house</h2><p>In a market with this many associations, the covenants matter as much as the house. Before you make an offer, read the HOA bylaws and rules, because they can restrict short-term and long-term rentals, pets, parking, golf carts, fences, and exterior changes. The classic trap: you have spent years training your dog to walk off leash, then buy into a community with a strict leash rule and lose the thing you valued most. Read the documents before you make an offer, not after.</p><p>Ask us and we will get you the governing documents for any Conway community, from our own files if we already have them or by requesting them for you if we do not. It is also why a Chapter3 agent asks a lot of questions about how you live. We would rather spend the time up front than match you to a home with a rule or an HOA that blocks what you enjoy or turns out to be a poor investment.</p><h2>Conway community stats</h2><p>Official figures from government sources, with no interpretation added. Each links to its source so you can read the full data yourself.</p><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.25rem 1.4rem;margin:1rem 0"><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.5rem">Population &amp; income &middot; <a href="https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/conwaycitysouthcarolina" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Census Bureau</a></p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Population (2020 Census)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">25,210</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Median household income (ACS)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about $56,650</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Persons in poverty (ACS)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 21%</span></div><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem">Cost, taxes &amp; risk &middot; government sources</p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Median owner-occupied home value (ACS)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about $287,700 &nbsp;<a href="https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/conwaycitysouthcarolina" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Census</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Property assessment ratio</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">4% primary / 6% second home &nbsp;<a href="https://dor.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC DOR</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Flood hazard by address</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://msc.fema.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FEMA Flood Map Service Center</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Crime statistics</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://cde.ucr.cjis.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBI Crime Data Explorer</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.sled.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SLED</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">School performance</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Dept. of Education report cards</a></span></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.7">How to pull the crime numbers yourself: on the <a href="https://cde.ucr.cjis.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBI Crime Data Explorer</a>, choose South Carolina, then select the local agency to see reported offenses by year. The local agency is the county sheriff's office or the city police department. On <a href="https://www.sled.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SLED</a>, open the current "Crime in South Carolina" report and find the county and agency tables. Chapter3 will pull the current figures for a specific address with you.</p><h2>Conway school stats</h2><p>Public schools fall within the Conway attendance area of Horry County Schools. Conway is also home to Coastal Carolina University and Horry-Georgetown Technical College. Figures are from the South Carolina Department of Education report card and federal education data, with no interpretation added.</p><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.25rem 1.4rem;margin:1rem 0"><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.5rem">Conway High School &middot; <a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC report card</a></p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Grades served</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">9-12</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Enrollment</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 1,680</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Math proficiency</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">56% (state average 43%)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Reading and writing proficiency</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">63% (state average 52%)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Student-to-teacher ratio</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">18 to 1</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Federal support designation</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">Additional Targeted Support (ATSI)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">State report card rating</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">see current rating</a></span></div></div><p>For the elementary and middle schools that feed it, and for any exact address, read the full report cards at the <a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Carolina Department of Education</a>. The report card also publishes SAT and ACT averages, Advanced Placement and dual-enrollment participation, college-and-career readiness, dropout rate, and chronic absenteeism. Ask your Chapter3 agent and we will pull the report card for the school an address is zoned to.</p><h2>Who Conway suits</h2><p>Conway fits long-term-rental investors who want steady student and workforce demand rather than seasonal swings. It also fits primary-residence buyers who want more house and land for the money than the beach towns offer, and buyers who value historic character and a real downtown. It is a growth-corridor, appreciation, and cash-flow market, not a vacation-rental market, and we will position it that way rather than sell it as something it is not.</p></div>
